# pathtracer scene v1
#
# Three solids of optical glass, raked by one slab out of frame behind them.
#
#   Pathtracer --scene scenes/prism.pts --sky 0 --bounces 16 --samples 4096 --capture prism.png
#
# Two of them are dense flint at Abbe 26 and one is crown at Abbe 64, so the colour fringing
# along their edges and in what they throw is a direct read-out of the one number that differs.
# Shapes were chosen for what they do to light rather than for variety: the cube stood on edge
# is a prism and deflects, the sphere is a lens and focuses to a point, the cylinder is a lens
# in one axis and focuses to a line.
#
# Where the focus lands is set by the index alone -- the back focus of a sphere sits
# n*R/(2(n-1)) from its centre -- which is why there is no diamond here. At 2.417 that figure
# is 0.77 against a radius of 0.9, so a diamond sphere crosses over *inside itself* and is
# spreading again by the time the light leaves. It is the most brilliant thing you can put in
# front of a camera and it casts almost nothing.
#
# A caveat worth setting expectations with: the caustics here are subtle, and that is the
# renderer rather than the scene. A shadow ray passes straight through transmissive geometry by
# design (see anyhit_shadow_mesh), so glass casts no shadow at all -- the floor under these
# objects is lit exactly as if they were not there. In life a caustic is bright against the
# shadow of the thing that made it, and here there is no shadow to be bright against, so it has
# to compete with fully lit floor. Raking the light across the floor buys some of that contrast
# back, by cutting the direct lighting with the cosine at the surface while doing nothing to the
# refracted cone, which is focused and does not care what angle it arrived at. It does not buy
# all of it. To go further you have to block the direct light with real geometry: an opaque
# panel between lamp and floor, with the glass sitting in a gap in it, is the setup that gives
# the textbook picture.
#
# The camera is pitched down far enough that the floor fills the frame and the horizon sits at
# the top edge. That is not framing for its own sake -- it is what makes the scene work. Glass
# has no appearance of its own: it is only ever a picture of whatever is behind and around it,
# so a transparent object against a black background is a black object. The lit floor is what
# the glass has to show, and it is also the surface anything it throws lands on, so pointing
# the camera at it solves both at once. There is deliberately no back wall.
#
# The floor is mid grey rather than white for the same reason: on a white floor there is no
# headroom left to be brighter with.
#
# The lamp is an emissive slab rather than a `light` block, and for the caustics that is not
# optional. Explicit lights are not in the acceleration structure, so a bounce ray can never
# find one, and a direct contribution that had to cross glass is dropped rather than trusted --
# a point or area light gives a lit floor and nothing focused at all. An emissive *instance* is
# found by BSDF rays, which is the shape of a caustic path: floor, into the glass, out of the
# glass, into the lamp. That needs four bounces before it reaches anything and more once it
# starts reflecting internally, hence --bounces 16.
#
# It is also the one path next event estimation cannot help with -- the vertex before the light
# is a delta, so there is no direction to connect toward -- so those paths are found by chance
# and are much the noisiest thing here. Everything else in the frame is sampled explicitly and
# settles quickly.
#
# If the whole frame comes out too hot or too flat, reach for --exposure before editing any
# number in here.

camera
  position 0 3.2 7.2
  yaw 180
  pitch -20
  fov 40

entity "ground"
  shape       plane
  translation 0 0 0
  rotation    0 0 0
  scale       30 1 30
  albedo      0.45 0.45 0.47
  roughness   1

# Dense flint (SF11), stood on one edge -- a unit cube turned 45 degrees about Z is a prism,
# and its half-diagonal is 1.13, which is where the y translation comes from. Abbe 26 is about
# as dispersive as ordinary optical glass gets.
entity "prism"
  shape        cube
  translation  0 1.14 0
  rotation     0 0 45
  scale        0.8 0.8 0.8
  albedo       1 1 1
  roughness    0
  transmission 1
  ior          1.785
  abbe         26

# Dense flint again, as a sphere, because a sphere is a lens and this is the one that throws a
# real caustic. Where the focus lands is what decides that, and it is set by the index alone:
# the back focus of a sphere sits n*R/(2(n-1)) from its centre, which for flint at 1.785 is
# 1.02 against a radius of 0.9 -- just outside the glass, so the cone converges on the floor a
# little in front of it. Diamond was here first and was a mistake: at 2.417 that same figure is
# 0.77, *inside* the sphere, so the light has already crossed over and is spreading again by
# the time it leaves. A diamond sphere is brilliant to look at and casts almost nothing.
entity "flint sphere"
  shape        sphere
  translation  -2.4 0.9 0.3
  rotation     0 0 0
  scale        0.9 0.9 0.9
  albedo       1 1 1
  roughness    0
  transmission 1
  ior          1.785
  abbe         26

# Crown glass, and a cylinder rather than a sphere: a cylinder is a lens in one axis only, so
# it focuses to a *line* instead of a point and lays a bright streak across the floor rather
# than a spot. Abbe 64 against the flint's 26 is the comparison the scene is really for -- same
# light, same geometry, four times less colour separation in what it throws.
entity "crown cylinder"
  shape        cylinder
  translation  2.4 0.9 0.3
  rotation     0 0 0
  scale        0.7 0.9 0.7
  albedo       1 1 1
  roughness    0
  transmission 1
  ior          1.517
  abbe         64

# The only light. Behind the group and above it, tilted so its underside faces them, and
# parked well above the camera's own horizontal -- which at this pitch is the top edge of
# frame -- so it lights the scene without appearing in it.
#
# Behind rather than in front, which is the difference between a picture of some glass and a
# picture of what glass does: lit from the front, the caustics fall away from the camera and
# hide behind the objects casting them. Lit from behind they are thrown forward into open
# floor, and the objects are rimmed and lit through at the same time.
#
# Low as well as behind, and that is worth explaining because it costs a lot of brightness --
# hence the large emission_strength against a slab this size. A shadow ray passes straight
# through transmissive geometry here by design (see anyhit_shadow_mesh), so glass casts no
# shadow at all: the floor under these objects is lit exactly as if they were not there, and a
# caustic has to be bright enough to read on top of that rather than inside a shadow the way
# it would in life. Raking the light across the floor is what buys the contrast back. It cuts
# the direct lighting by the cosine at the surface while doing nothing to the refracted cone,
# which is focused and does not care what angle it arrived at.
#
# It also has to stay above the camera's own horizontal, which at this pitch is the top edge of
# frame -- at 4.2 it clears it, at 3 it does not and the slab appears in shot.
#
# Black albedo because it is a lamp, not a lit surface.
entity "lamp"
  shape             cube
  translation       -1 4.2 -6
  rotation          -61 0 0
  scale             0.85 0.04 0.85
  albedo            0 0 0
  emission          1 0.97 0.92
  emission_strength 130
  roughness         1
